THE UNIT, in full:

{
 "period_number": 17,
 "period_duration_minutes": 40,
 "progression_stage": 6,
 "stage_label": "Synthesise: Apply the Full Framework to Distinguish Living from Non-living",
 "synthesis": true,
 "roles": [
  {
   "actor": "Teacher",
   "role_description": "Presents the four ambiguous cases and facilitates a structured justification-sharing discussion, pressing students on the quality of their characteristic-by-characteristic evidence."
  },
  {
   "actor": "Students (Individual)",
   "role_description": "Applies the full seven-characteristic framework to each case in writing, produces a classification decision with a structured justification, and then discusses with the class."
  }
 ],
 "activity_title": "The Tide Pool Census: Reading Life Where You Do Not Expect It",
 "activity_description": "Students receive a printed data card describing seven objects found in a shallow coastal tide pool — a hermit crab inside a borrowed shell, a sea anemone attached to a rock, a clump of seaweed, a barnacle cemented to a stone, a fragment of dry coral skeleton, a small fish darting between rocks, and a pebble — along with one short observation note for each object. Working first alone and then in pairs, students complete a structured evidence grid that tests each object against every shared life characteristic from the chapter, then use the grid to place each object on a 'Certainty Line' drawn on a shared sheet: from 'definitely living' through 'needs more evidence' to 'definitely non-living'. The pair then chooses one object from the uncertain middle zone and writes a paragraph explaining exactly which characteristics could resolve the uncertainty and what observation or experiment would supply the missing evidence. The unit closes with a whole-class reveal in which pairs report their middle-zone object and the class builds a single agreed census, naming the chapter's concepts as the tools that made the sorting possible.",
 "teacher_notes": "This synthesis unit asks students to apply the full seven-characteristic framework to seven tide pool objects, moving from individual evidence grids through a paired Certainty Line negotiation to a whole-class census. The hermit crab and barnacle are the richest cases for pressing students past surface appearances — when pairs disagree on Certainty Line placement, require each to name their strongest characteristic-level evidence rather than repeat their conclusion. Object descriptions and the evidence grid are prepared before the lesson (see materials); the accept-list of key teaching points for each object lives in the visual aid 'Tide pool object profiles'.",
 "materials": [
  "Printed data card (one per student) listing seven tide pool objects with two-sentence observation notes each (see visual aid: 'Tide pool object profiles')",
  "Evidence grid worksheet (one per student) (see visual aid: 'Evidence grid layout')",
  "A4 sheet (one per pair) with a horizontal Certainty Line pre-drawn and labelled 'Definitely Living' at the left, 'Needs More Evidence' in the centre, and 'Definitely Non-living' at the right",
  "Small sticky notes or pen for marking object positions on the Certainty Line",
  "Blackboard or whiteboard for the whole-class census tally"
 ],
 "time_bands": [
  {
   "minutes": "0-5",
   "activity": "Teacher distributes the data card and reads the tide pool scene aloud: a rocky shelf exposed briefly by low tide, holding seven objects. Teacher names each object and its observation note, then asks: 'Before you write anything — which of these do you think is the hardest to classify, and why?' Two or three students respond briefly. Teacher does not confirm or correct; instead says: 'Your grid will tell you — let's find out.'"
  },
  {
   "minutes": "5-18",
   "activity": "Students work individually to complete the evidence grid. For each object they tick, cross, or write a question mark in each characteristic column, then record a verdict in the final column. Teacher circulates, pausing at students who have marked the barnacle or hermit crab as straightforwardly non-living and asking: 'Which characteristic are you least sure about for this one? What would you need to see?' No answers are given; questions are left open."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "18-28",
   "activity": "Students pair up and compare grids. Together they place all seven objects on the Certainty Line on their shared A4 sheet, negotiating any disagreements. Each pair then selects one object from the middle 'Needs More Evidence' zone and writes a short paragraph (four to six sentences) naming: which characteristic(s) produced the uncertainty, what specific observation or simple test would resolve it, and what verdict they predict the test would give and why."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "28-38",
   "activity": "Whole-class census: teacher draws seven object names on the board. Pairs report their Certainty Line position for each object; teacher marks the range of responses. For any object where pairs disagree, one pair from each camp states their strongest evidence. The class votes on a final verdict. Teacher records the agreed census column by column, reading aloud the characteristic that clinched each contested verdict."
  },
  {
   "minutes": "38-40",
   "activity": "Teacher closes by pointing to the completed census and naming the chapter's framework directly: 'Every decision you just made used one or more of seven characteristics — movement, growth, nutrition, respiration, excretion, sensitivity, reproduction — and the understanding that a living thing can be at any stage of its life cycle, including a stage where some characteristics are temporarily hidden. That framework is what made a tide pool census possible for a class that has never studied the sea. That is what this chapter gave you.'"
  }
 ],
 "homework": [],
 "pedagogical_approach": "Inquiry approach — students construct evidence-based arguments from data rather than recalling facts; the tide pool setting is novel throughout the chapter and the vehicle (evidence grid feeding into a positional Certainty Line, followed by a targeted 'missing evidence' paragraph) appears in none of the final units of the shorter plans.",
 "visual_aids": [
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "title": "Tide pool object profiles",
   "text": "Write each description as two sentences on the printed data card so students must reason rather than read off a verdict.\n\n1. HERMIT CRAB — Living, but hidden inside a borrowed shell that is non-living. This is the richest case: it directly echoes the chapter's germination logic — capacity not currently visible does not mean absent — without reusing the chapter's own shell narrative.\n\n2. SEA ANEMONE — Living, attached to a rock. Lack of obvious locomotion may mislead students; steer them toward sensitivity and nutrition as the decisive characteristics.\n\n3. CLUMP OF SEAWEED — Living. Straightforward once students apply nutrition (photosynthesis) and growth.\n\n4. BARNACLE — The most productive uncertain case. Cemented still, showing no obvious movement, but it feeds, respires, and reproduces. Steer pairs who pick it toward asking which single observation would be most decisive.\n\n5. FRAGMENT OF DRY CORAL SKELETON — Intentionally non-living. It is the residue of a once-living organism; students may debate whether past life counts. Hold firm: the chapter's definition requires current exhibition of characteristics.\n\n6. SMALL FISH — Living. Intended as the clearest anchor case on the 'Definitely Living' end.\n\n7. PEBBLE — Non-living. Intended as the clearest anchor case on the 'Definitely Non-living' end.\n\nIn the closing band, name all seven characteristics explicitly as you consolidate — movement, growth, nutrition, respiration, excretion, sensitivity, reproduction — so closure does not remain at the level of 'we sorted the objects'. If the class has already closed the chapter in a previous lesson, open by telling students the tide pool is a place where the same tools they already have meet an entirely unfamiliar cast of organisms — the point is not new knowledge but sharpened use."
  },
  {
   "type": "table",
   "title": "Evidence grid layout",
   "table": "Object | Movement | Growth | Nutrition | Respiration | Excretion | Sensitivity | Reproduction | Verdict\nHermit crab | | | | | | | |\nSea anemone | | | | | | | |\nSeaweed | | | | | | | |\nBarnacle | | | | | | | |\nDry coral skeleton | | | | | | | |\nSmall fish | | | | | | | |\nPebble | | | | | | | |"
  }
 ]
}

======================================================================
FOUNDER DIRECTION FOR THIS UNIT (2026-08-18): the primary aid must BE the student-facing printed data card as a TABLE — Object | Observation notes (exactly two sentences per object, written so students must reason: no verdict words, no characteristic named as an answer). Author these fourteen sentences; they are the licensed teacher-prepared blank. The teacher key becomes a SECOND, separate table — Object | Intended verdict | Decisive characteristic | one-line steer — replacing the current prose essay entirely (its design intent survives as those columns; its repetition of the teacher notes does not). Keep the evidence-grid aid as is.

Reorganise it now, as the single JSON object specified.
